<Layout title="Ideas | Roy Li">
  <main>
    <section class="page-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Ideas</div><h1>Common sense principles for British Columbia.</h1><p class="lead">Roy’s views are grounded in common sense conservative principles: public safety, fiscal responsibility, less red tape, entrepreneurship, and respect for families and taxpayers.</p></section>
    <section class="content">
      <h2>1. Common sense conservative government</h2><p>British Columbia needs a government that is more prudent, more disciplined, and more respectful of limits. That means fewer ideological experiments, less bureaucracy, more attention to outcomes, and a willingness to say no when policies do not work.</p>
      <h2>2. Balance the budget</h2><p>Balanced budgets are not just accounting. They are a moral commitment to the future. When government spends beyond its means, ordinary people eventually pay through higher taxes, weaker services, or fewer opportunities.</p>
      <h2>3. Restore public safety</h2><p>Public safety is not a luxury. It is the basic promise of government. Families should feel safe in their neighbourhoods. Seniors should feel safe in public spaces. Small businesses should not be left alone to absorb disorder, crime, and repeat offences.</p>
      <h2>4. Cut red tape and support enterprise</h2><p>Roy is a long-term entrepreneur. He knows that business confidence is fragile. Government should make it easier to invest, hire, build, and stay in British Columbia. A province that punishes enterprise with delay, paperwork, and uncertainty eventually loses jobs, capital, and hope.</p>
      <h2>5. Work for people, not bureaucrats</h2><p>Citizens deserve plain language and real accountability. What is the problem? What is the cost? Who pays? What result should we expect? Good government works for people, not bureaucrats, and starts by answering those questions honestly.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
